Package: hoard
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         python3 (>= 3.11),
         python3-cryptography,
         python3-cryptography (>= 44~) | python3-argon2
Recommends: xclip | wl-clipboard
Description: encrypted password vault in one readable file
 hoard keeps passwords in a single file you can decrypt without hoard. The
 header carrying the Argon2id parameters, the salt and the nonce is plaintext,
 so anyone can read how the vault was made, and it is authenticated as
 associated data, so nobody can weaken those parameters without invalidating
 the tag.
 .
 Fresh salt and fresh nonce on every write, contents sealed with
 ChaCha20-Poly1305, and the file format documented so the vault outlives this
 program.
 .
 Argon2id comes from python3-cryptography where that is new enough to have it,
 and from python3-argon2 otherwise. The two produce identical keys, so a vault
 moves between machines regardless of which one is installed.
 .
 This package contains the command line interface.

Package: hoard-gui
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         hoard (= ${binary:Version}),
         python3-argon2,
         python3-gi,
         gir1.2-gtk-3.0
Description: encrypted password vault in one readable file (GTK interface)
 hoard keeps passwords in a single file you can decrypt without hoard, using
 Argon2id and ChaCha20-Poly1305.
 .
 This package contains a GTK window for the vault. It follows the system theme
 rather than shipping its own colours, so it looks like the rest of the desktop
 instead of like a brand. Every cryptographic operation is done by the hoard
 package; nothing in the window derives a key or touches a cipher.
 .
 It depends on python3-argon2 specifically, rather than accepting either
 backend the way the command line tool does. The window derives keys on a
 worker thread so it can keep repainting, and cryptography's Argon2id holds the
 GIL for the whole derivation, which would stop the interface anyway. argon2-cffi
 releases it.
